  "subRow": null,
  "note": "A relief in line (GSP 1947 Inf Div in Battle s29 pp 134-140): a Bde in defence relieved by an incoming Bde, which takes over all its responsibilities while the relieved Bde is withdrawn (para 1 p 134). The posns are the Bde two up as it stands (the Bde Def two up layout: two Bns forward, one in depth, OoW Vol I Annexes B-D). A Bde Gp needs at least 48 h for the planning and conduct of a relief; the warning order gives the times, the nights, the priorities on the routes, the size of the advance parties and the order in which the units are relieved (para 4 pp 134-135). Recce by day of the existing defence and its obstacles, the relief routes, the assembly areas, the debussing and turn-round area and the admin installations (para 5 f p 137): each incoming Bn has an Assy area behind, a relief route forward and a guides RV at the posn it takes over (their places E); one debus and turn-round point (E). The relief is by stages, rear to front or front to rear (para 5 d p 136); command changes as late as possible, once communications work, normally when more than half is relieved, the forward Bns relieved (para 5 e p 137); the relieved artillery stays until the forward units are relieved (para 5 k p 138); by night as a rule, normal activity kept up for secrecy (paras 5 c, g, h pp 135-137); a joint HQ during the relief (para 5 m p 139).",
